    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Haematolymphoid neoplasms of the female genital tract (FGT) {28803429; 16224219; 28284518; 11444509}</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "",
    "copyright": "Adapted from Kurman RJ, Carcangiu ML, Herrington CS, Young RH (Eds.)2014: WHO Classification of Tumours of Femal eReproductive Organs (4th ed.). IARC: Lyon.",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"0\" width=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 3.5pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 155.95pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" colspan=\"2\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Type of lymphoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Frequency</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Cellular composition</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Usual immunophenotype<sup>a</sup></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Genetic features<sup>a</sup></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 703.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" colspan=\"7\" width=\"938\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>B-cell lymphomas</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Most common type of lymphoma throughout the FGT</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Centroblasts, immunoblasts, spindle cells, and/or anaplastic large B&nbsp;cells; sclerosis is common, especially in cervix and vagina</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Monotypic sIg+, CD20+, BCL6+/&minus;, CD10&minus;/+, BCL2+/&minus;, IRF4&nbsp;(MUM1)+/&minus;, CD43+/&minus;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">IGH clonal; <em>MYD88</em> p.Leu265Pro and/or <em>CD79B</em> p.Tyr196 mutations in a subset</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Poorly differentiated carcinoma; florid reactive lymphoid hyperplasia (lymphoma-like lesion), especially in cervix; spindle cell sarcoma; sex cord tumour in cases with cord-like growth</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Follicular lymphoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Second most common type of FGT lymphoma; mainly occurs in ovaries and cervix</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Mixture of centrocytes and centroblasts, follicular dendritic cells; sclerosis is common</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Monotypic sIg+, CD20+, CD10+, BCL6+, CD5&minus;, CD43&minus;, cyclin&nbsp;D1&minus;, BCL2&nbsp;+ or &minus;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">IGH clonal; t(14;18) (IGH-<em>BCL2</em>) found in some cases</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Chronic inflammatory process</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Burkitt lymphoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Third most common type of FGT lymphoma; mainly occurs in ovaries</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Medium-sized atypical lymphoid cells with round nuclei, coarse chromatin, basophilic cytoplasm, numerous mitoses, tingible-body macrophages</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Monotypic sIgM+, CD20+, CD10+, BCL6+, BCL2&minus;, MYC+, Ki-67&nbsp;~100%</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">IGH clonal; t(8;14), t(2;8), or (8;22) (<em>MYC</em>); endemic cases: EBV+; sporadic and immunodeficiency-associated cases: minority EBV+</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Other high-grade lymphomas</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT lymphoma)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Uncommon type of FGT lymphoma; most common in endometrium; rare in other sites</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Marginal zone B&nbsp;cells, small lymphocytes, plasma cells, reactive follicles, lymphoepithelial lesions; in endometrium, nodular growth without residual reactive follicles</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Monotypic sIg+, cIg+/&minus; (IgM&nbsp;&gt; IgG&nbsp;&gt; IgA), CD20+, CD5&minus;, CD10&minus;, BCL6&minus;, BCL2+, CD43&minus;/+, cyclin&nbsp;D1&minus;; in endometrium, CD43+, CD5&minus;/+</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">IGH clonal; translocations of <em>MALT1</em> and IGH not described in FGT MALT lymphoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Chronic inflammatory process</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">B-lymphoblastic leukaemia/lymphoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Rare; more often secondary than primary in the FGT</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Small to medium-sized blasts with scant cytoplasm</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">CD19+, CD20&minus;, TdT+, CD10+, CD3&minus;, MPO&minus;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">IGH clonal; other changes variable</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Other high-grade lymphomas; small round cell tumours</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Intravascular large B-cell lymphoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Rare type of large B-cell lymphoma that can involve the FGT</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Centroblasts, immunoblasts</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">CD20+, subset CD5+, usually non-GCB phenotype</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">IGH clonal</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Leukaemia; carcinoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 703.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" colspan=\"7\" width=\"938\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>T-cell and NK/T-cell lymphomas</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Peripheral T-cell lymphoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Rare</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Variable; small, medium-sized, and/or large cells</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Variable expression of pan&ndash;T-cell antigens; often loss of one or more T-cell antigens; CD4 or CD8 often +</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">TR clonal; other changes variable</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">For low-grade cases, chronic inflammatory process; for high-grade cases, other poorly differentiated neoplasms</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">T-lymphoblastic leukaemia/lymphoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Rare; more often secondary than primary in the FGT</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Small to medium-sized blasts with round, irregular, or convoluted nuclei; finely dispersed to dark chromatin; variably prominent nucleoli; scant cytoplasm; frequent mitoses</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">CD3+, CD7+, TdT+, CD1a+, CD10+/&minus;, often CD4/CD8 double +</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">TR clonal; IGH clonal in a minority</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Other high-grade lymphomas; small round cell tumours</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal-type</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Very rare</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Small, medium-sized, and/or large atypical lymphoid cells; necrosis; vascular damage</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">cCD3+, CD2+, CD5&minus;, CD56+, granzyme&nbsp;B+, perforin+</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">NK-lineage cases: TR germline, EBV+</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Other high-grade lymphomas; inflammatory process if necrosis is extensive and viable lymphoma is sparse</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 703.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" colspan=\"7\" width=\"938\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Hodgkin lymphoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Classic Hodgkin lymphoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Vanishingly rare</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Reed&ndash;Sternberg cells and variants in a reactive background</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">CD15+/&minus;, CD30+, CD20&minus;/+, PAX5+, IRF4&nbsp;(MUM1)+, CD3&minus;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">EBV+ in ~40% of cases</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Other high-grade lymphomas if tumour cells are abundant; inflammatory process if tumour cells are scarce</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 703.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" colspan=\"7\" width=\"938\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Myeloid neoplasms</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 14.15pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"19\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 141.8pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"189\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Acute myeloid leukaemia, myeloid sarcoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 99.2pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"132\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Rare</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.0pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Variably sized, usually medium-sized blasts with fine chromatin and nucleoli; maturing myeloid elements admixed in some cases</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Lysozyme+, CD68+/&minus;, MPO+/&minus;, CD34+/&minus;, CD33+, KIT&nbsp;(CD117)+/&minus;, CD43+, CD20&minus;, CD3&minus;, CAE+</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Varied</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 112.05pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"149\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Large cell lymphoma; carcinoma, especially metastasis from the breast; extramedullary haematopoiesis</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">cIg, cytoplasmic immunoglobulin; GCB, germinal-centre B&nbsp;cell; MPO, myeloperoxidase; sIg, surface immunoglobulin.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup>a</sup>Most data are derived from neoplasms outside the female genital tract.</p>",
